## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical multi-task learning under streaming constraints, including feature hashing, online updates, drift detection, calibration, and retraining cadence.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-task learning with shared representations
- Online learning with partial_fit
- Feature hashing for high-cardinality categoricals
- Drift detection (PSI, drift in AUC)
- Latency and memory constraints, calibration

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: shared feature extractor feeds two logistic heads
class MultiTaskLR {
  constructor() {}
  update(features, y1, y2) {}
  predict(features) { return {p1:0.5, p2:0.5} }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to a third task with a different label delay?
- How would you validate drift alarms in production and trigger retraining bursts?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests system design for real-time fraud scoring at scale with privacy constraints, multi-tenant data sharing, and safe rollbacks.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming feature store and time-window features
- Hybrid inference paths: server-side and on-device
- Federated learning with differential privacy
- Drift detection and canary-based rollout strategies
- Model calibration and per-transaction explainability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo scoring sketch
function score(tx, globalModel, localModel){
  const feats = extract(tx)
  const s1 = globalModel.predict(feats)
  const s2 = localModel.predict(feats)
  return calibrate((0.6*s1 + 0.4*s2));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle label latency for online updates?
- What metrics trigger rollback and how would you test rollbacks?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests privacy-preserving on-device ML, multimodal fusion, and efficient latency. Evaluates understanding of FL/DP, quantization, and on-device retrieval with drift handling.

## Key Concepts
- On-device inference and latency budgeting
- Multimodal embedding fusion
- Federated learning with secure aggregation and differential privacy
- Approximate nearest neighbors for retrieval

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo on-device inference sketch
function inference(text, image) {
  const t = textEncoder.encode(text);
  const i = imageEncoder.encode(image);
  const z = fuse(t, i);
  return topK(z, 5);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor and respond to drift in user preferences?
- How would you handle new modalities or cold-start users without central data access?
